<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Amazing Community – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Amazing Community</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> This School Has Been Here a Long Time!</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Exchange opinions about familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe a community tradition using simple language and time expressions (e.g., 'In our town, we have a music festival every year, like the Panama Jazz Festival. It takes place in January.' 'La Mejorana Festival happens in Guararé, Los Santos Province, usually in September.').
Can exchange opinions on community topics (e.g., 'I like our park. Do you?').
Can share information about community events (e.g., 'What time is the music festival?' 'It begins at 2:00 p.m.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;"><h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Stage & Description</h4></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;"><h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Activities & Teacher Actions</h4></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;"><h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Estimated Date and Time</h4></th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of different community places (e.g., an old school, a new park, a bustling market, a quiet library) and asks students to identify them. Students then choose their favorite place. Example prompts: "What do you see in the pictures?" "Which place do you like the most?" "Why?" (e.g., "I like the park because it's beautiful and friendly.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models expressing opinions and asking simple questions about community places and roles. For example: "I like our school because it is old and has many traditions." (Teacher points to an old school picture). "Do you like our park? Why or why not?" The teacher emphasizes clear pronunciation, focusing on the stress in compound nouns like 'neighborhood' (stress on 'neigh-') and distinguishing short /ʊ/ in 'cook' from long /uː/ in 'school'. The teacher says: "Listen: 'cook' /kʊk/, 'school' /skuːl'."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of 'opinion' and 'familiar topics'.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'The school is red' an opinion or a fact?" (Fact)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'I think the school is beautiful' an opinion or a fact?" (Opinion)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we have different opinions about the same thing?" (Yes)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Amazing Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue between two 6th-grade students, Carlos and Maria, discussing a local community festival and their school's history. The dialogue will incorporate the target grammar (present simple, past simple, present perfect) and vocabulary, such as 'community', 'festival', 'traditions', 'market', 'cook', 'celebrate', 'friendly', 'yesterday', 'every year', 'has worked'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Dialogue Snippet:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Carlos:</b> "Hi Maria! Our community has a big music festival every year, like the Panama Jazz Festival. It takes place in January. Do you like it?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Maria:</b> "Yes, I really enjoy it! People gather at the park before the festival. Yesterday, I visited the market. What delicious food do people cook there?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Carlos:</b> "Oh, they cook fresh and tasty empanadas! My grandmother is a vendor there; she has worked at the market for many years. Our school also has many traditions, it has been here a long time!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Maria:</b> "That's cool! How long has our teacher worked here?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student can understand and produce short, simple spoken texts to exchange opinions and information about familiar topics related to their community and school.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What are Carlos and Maria talking about?" (Their community festival and school.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The music festival happens in December." (False) "Maria's grandmother is a teacher." (False)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match phrases like "Music festival" with "January" or "Grandmother" with "vendor."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Practice using present simple for routines/facts, past simple for past events, and present perfect for duration, all within the context of community roles, events, and traditions. Focus on vocabulary: 'community', 'event', 'park', 'festival', 'traditions', 'market', 'teacher', 'farmer', 'vendor', 'artist', 'cook', 'celebrate', 'share', 'friendly', 'delicious', 'every year', 'yesterday', 'last month', 'How long has...?'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Controlled Practice – Sentence Completion (Oral):</b> The teacher provides sentence stems on the board related to community activities or roles, and students complete them orally with appropriate vocabulary and grammar. (e.g., "A ___ helps keep our community safe." -> "A police officer helps keep our community safe." "Our community ___ a big festival ___." -> "Our community celebrates a big festival every year.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Semi-controlled Practice – Q&A Chain Drill:</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher starts by asking a question (e.g., "What delicious food do people cook at the market?"). The first student answers (e.g., "They cook fresh empanadas.") and then asks the next student a new question using target grammar/vocabulary (e.g., "What does your neighbor do?"). Students continue the chain, practicing asking and answering questions in present simple. The teacher reminds them to pronounce compound nouns correctly (e.g., 'neighborhood') and short/long vowels.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Pair Interaction – Sentence Builders:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides sentence frames or prompts for exchanging opinions and information about their community.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Partner A: "I like our [park/library/school] because..."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Partner B: "I agree/disagree. I think..." or "Our community has a [festival/event]. It takes place in [month/season]."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Partner A: "That sounds [engaging/fun]! How long has your family lived in this [neighborhood]?" (Emphasize 'neighborhood' pronunciation).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Partner B: "We have lived here for [number] years."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-20 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Amazing Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Partner Interview & Share. Students will conduct a short interview with a classmate, pretending their classmate is an older community member or a local historian. They will ask questions about community traditions, events, and their school's history.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students form pairs. Each pair receives an 'Interview Card' with prompts and space for notes.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A interviews Student B (the 'community elder'). Student A uses questions like: "What traditions does our community celebrate?" "When does the [festival] take place?" "What is your favorite community event?" "What do you think about our school? Has it changed a lot?" "How long have you lived in this neighborhood?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B responds using simple language, present and past simple, and present perfect. They also express opinions (e.g., "I like our old school; it's beautiful. It has been here for 50 years.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students swap roles.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After the interviews, pairs briefly share one interesting tradition or opinion they discussed with another pair or with the whole class. (e.g., "My partner said our community has a special 'Day of the Farmer' event in October. She thinks it's very important.") The teacher reminds students to connect, discuss, and share information.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the partner interviews and share-out. The teacher will circulate and note down instances of target language use, communication effectiveness, and pronunciation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the partner interview and then share one piece of information/opinion with the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Students pronounce target vocabulary (e.g., 'neighborhood', 'cook', 'school') clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Students use at least 5-7 words from the lesson's vocabulary list (e.g., 'community', 'festival', 'traditions', 'market', 'enjoy', 'friendly').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> Students exchange opinions and information clearly using simple sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Students actively listen and respond to their partner's questions and opinions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar accuracy:</b> Students attempt to use present simple, past simple, and present perfect forms to describe events and duration.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short written reflection before leaving the class.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I learned about our community's traditions today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One question I still have about our school or community is..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of talking about our community did I find easy today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part was difficult, and what can I do to improve my speaking?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The speaking activities worked well to encourage interaction and opinion exchange, especially the Q&A chain drill and partner interviews. Students engaged with vocabulary related to community roles and events. Some students struggled with forming present perfect questions (e.g., 'How long has...'), requiring more explicit modeling and practice in future lessons. Pronunciation of compound nouns like 'neighborhood' and distinguishing short/long vowels needs continued emphasis. I need to prepare more varied scenarios for expressing agreement/disagreement next time to deepen opinion exchange.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4, students will use the vocabulary and grammatical structures practiced today to write short paragraphs describing community events, traditions, and their opinions about them. They will transform their spoken ideas into written form, building on the fluency gained today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>